  <p>The pattern is old and it is consistent. An image offered as evidence has to be evidence.</p>

  <h2>Can product screens be simulated in ads?</h2>

  <p>Not in a way that passes a render off as what the device shows. The rulings read for this page do not turn on the words "screen images simulated." They turn on a narrower question: does the ad present the image as the device's own output?</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A UK advertising regulator upheld a complaint against a dash cam brand's web page after finding that a night image of readable number plates was a stock photo with the plates added, not footage from the cameras advertised. Consumers, it ruled, would read the image as a screen grab from the product, and it told the advertiser not to use images that exaggerate product performance.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: UK advertising regulator ruling, 5 June 2024, an upheld investigation under the code's misleading advertising, substantiation and exaggeration rules.</p>

  <p>The same regulator's guidance deals with small print. It names a gameplay disclaimer, not a screen disclaimer, so applying it to a "screen images simulated" line is an analogy. It is a close one.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same regulator's guidance says images should not be altered in a way that exaggerates what the product can do, and that a qualification or small print such as "Not representative of actual gameplay" is generally unlikely to stop an ad from misleading.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: UK advertising regulator guidance on misleading imagery, 11 September 2025, published advice drawing on six earlier rulings.</p>

  <p>US law drew the same line long before.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The US Supreme Court held that an undisclosed mock-up in a TV demonstration was a material deceptive practice because it told viewers they were seeing proof of the claim with their own eyes. Where an ad rests on the seller's word rather than the viewer's own perception, the Court said, an undisclosed prop is not prohibited.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: FTC v. Colgate-Palmolive Co., 380 U.S. 374, decided 5 April 1965, the Court's opinion on a televised shaving demonstration using plexiglass in place of sandpaper.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The US Federal Trade Commission judges an ad by its overall impression, including its visual imagery, and says written disclosures or fine print may be insufficient to correct a misleading representation.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: FTC Policy Statement on Deception, 14 October 1983, the Commission's published enforcement policy, read in full text.</p>

  <p>For the build, that sorts interface work into two kinds. This is production practice, not a rule. A render shown as interface design asks the viewer to take the seller's word. A frame shown as a screen grab, a camera sample or a live demo asks viewers to trust their own eyes, and it has to come off the device.</p>

  <h2>What evidence do claimed specs need?</h2>

  <p>Evidence in hand before the ad runs, in each of the three markets read.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The UK non-broadcast code requires marketers to hold documentary evidence for objective claims before an ad runs, and bars ads that mislead by exaggerating the capability or performance of a product. The broadcast code applies the same two rules to TV.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: UK advertising codes, non-broadcast rules 3.7 and 3.11 and broadcast rules 3.9 and 3.12, code text read on the regulator's site 10 September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A UK advertising regulator upheld a complaint against a portable cooler ad that claimed it could "Cool Any Room in Seconds," finding the advertiser supplied no evidence and the claim was unsubstantiated and misleading.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: UK advertising regulator ruling, 26 November 2025, an upheld investigation of a paid online display ad under the code's misleading advertising, substantiation and exaggeration rules.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The US Federal Trade Commission treats an objective claim made without a reasonable basis as deceptive, and where an ad says "tests prove," it expects the advertiser to hold at least the level of proof the ad claims.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: FTC Policy Statement Regarding Advertising Substantiation, 23 November 1984, the Commission's published enforcement policy.</p>

  <p>China writes the rule into statute, and puts a price on breaking it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's Advertising Law requires information about a product's performance, function, quality, ingredients and price to be accurate, clear and unambiguous, and requires cited data, statistics and survey results to be true, accurate and sourced. An ad counts as false where performance, function, specification or quality information does not match reality and materially affects the purchase, or where it relies on fabricated or unverifiable data.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Advertising Law of the People's Republic of China (Guanggao Fa), 2021 amendment, Articles 8, 11 and 28, statute text read in Chinese on 10 September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Publishing a false ad in China draws a fine of three to five times the advertising fee, or 200,000 to 1,000,000 yuan where the fee cannot be calculated or is clearly low, rising to five to ten times, or 1,000,000 to 2,000,000 yuan, for three or more violations within two years or serious circumstances.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Advertising Law of the People's Republic of China (Guanggao Fa), 2021 amendment, Article 55, statute text read in Chinese on 10 September 2026.</p>

  <p>The production practice that follows is simple to state. The test report is an input to the storyboard, not a check at the end. Every figure on a spec callout, a detail page or a voiceover line traces back to it, in every language version.</p>

  <h2>How should certification marks and energy labels appear?</h2>

  <p>As the product actually holds them, and not before. China's certification rules name advertising outright.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Products on China's compulsory certification catalog must be certified and carry the CCC mark before they leave the factory, are sold, imported or used in business. The certification holder must use the mark correctly on the product, its packaging, and in advertising and product introductions, and no one may forge, alter, misuse, buy or transfer a certification mark.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Provisions on Compulsory Product Certification (Qiangzhixing Chanpin Renzheng Guanli Guiding), quality inspection authority Order 117 as amended by market regulator Order 61 of 29 September 2022, Articles 2, 32 and 33, read on a Chinese market regulator's site 10 September 2026.</p>

  <p>The mark requirements were revised for 2024, so a mark file from an older campaign needs checking before it is reused.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's certification regulator revised its compulsory certification mark requirements in an announcement dated 8 August 2023, in force from 1 January 2024.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: national certification and accreditation administration, Announcement 2023 No. 12, announcement page read on a Chinese market regulator's site 10 September 2026, dates only.</p>

  <p>US rules bite earlier in the calendar, at the teaser stage.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Under US rules, marketing a radio frequency device includes advertising it. A device advertised or displayed before FCC authorization must carry a statement that it has not been authorized and may not be offered for sale or lease, or sold or leased, until authorization is obtained.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: 47 CFR 2.803, as amended 25 November 2025, eCFR text read on the Legal Information Institute mirror 10 September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">US rules let a radio device with a built-in display show its required labeling electronically, reachable in no more than three steps from the settings menu, with the FCC identifier or other information also on the packaging or a removable label. Devices authorized by supplier's declaration may carry the FCC logo, on a voluntary basis.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: 47 CFR 2.935 and 2.1074(b), 82 FR 50827 and 50829, 2 November 2017, eCFR text read on the Legal Information Institute mirror 10 September 2026.</p>

  <p>The EU mark carries a narrow meaning, and a caption should not stretch it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The CE marking is the manufacturer's declaration that a product meets EU requirements, affixed only after the correct conformity assessment. It does not mean the EU or any authority approved the product as safe.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: European Commission CE marking page, dated 25 October 2021, and the EU business portal page last checked 17 July 2026, both read 10 September 2026.</p>

  <p>Energy labels split by market.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Where a product on China's energy label catalog is sold online, the energy label must be shown in a conspicuous position on the main product information page. A label used in advertising may be scaled in proportion, and no one may forge or misuse an energy label or use one for false publicity.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Energy Efficiency Label Measures (Nengyuan Xiaolv Biaoshi Guanli Banfa), Order No. 35 of the national development and quality inspection authorities, published 29 February 2016, in force 1 June 2016, Articles 6 and 17, read on China's market regulator site.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Since 1 March 2021, TVs, monitors and signage displays sold in the EU carry an energy label on an A to G scale. Any visual advertisement for a specific display model, online included, must show its energy efficiency class and the range of classes on the label, and technical promotional material that describes the model's parameters must show its class.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: European Commission product page for electronic displays, read 10 September 2026, summarizing Delegated Regulation (EU) 2019/2013 under framework Regulation (EU) 2017/1369.</p>

  <p>The two rules are not the same. China mandates the label on the online listing and allows a scaled version in advertising, without requiring one in every ad. The EU puts the class and the range into any visual ad for a specific display model. A display launching in both markets needs its label asset built for both uses.</p>

  <p>Marketplaces also place these images in particular listing slots. The <a href="/resources/insights/tmall-product-image-requirements">Tmall product image requirements article</a> covers the published slot maps and how far they can be trusted.</p>

  <h2>Which parts of a tech launch can be generated, and which must be captured?</h2>

  <p>One test sorts every asset. Is it offered as evidence of what the device is and does, or as the world around it? The table applies that test. The split is production practice, and the last column names the rule that decides each row.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Asset</th>
          <th scope="col">Generate</th>
          <th scope="col">Capture</th>
          <th scope="col">The rule that decides</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Footage or stills offered as device output</th>
          <td>No</td>
          <td>Yes, from the device itself</td>
          <td>UK dash cam ruling, 2024; Colgate-Palmolive, 1965</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Performance demonstrations</th>
          <td>No</td>
          <td>Yes, backed by test evidence</td>
          <td>UK cooler ruling, 2025; FTC substantiation statement, 1984</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Hero product stills, macro and packaging</th>
          <td>Only from a model trained on the product</td>
          <td>Yes, and the reference for any generation</td>
          <td>Marketplace AI image rule, reported 2025</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">White-background image</th>
          <td>A clean cutout from a capture</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>Production practice</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Interface animation</th>
          <td>Yes, as CGI shown as interface design</td>
          <td>Where the ad offers the screen as proof</td>
          <td>Colgate-Palmolive, 1965; FTC deception statement, 1983</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Lifestyle scenes and environments</th>
          <td>Yes</td>
          <td>Optional</td>
          <td>UK AI guidance, 2026: the advertiser stays responsible</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">People, pets or objects reacting to the device</th>
          <td>Context only, never the product's behavior</td>
          <td>The product's behavior</td>
          <td>UK robot toy ruling, 2026</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Certification mark and energy label images</th>
          <td>No</td>
          <td>The issued mark or label, reproduced</td>
          <td>CCC provisions, Articles 32 and 33; energy label Measures, Article 17</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Spec callouts</th>
          <td>Layout only</td>
          <td>Figures from the test report</td>
          <td>Non-broadcast rule 3.7; Advertising Law, Article 11</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>One delivered launch shows the split inside a single film.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">One launch film for an AI wearable became 22 cuts: a 90-second hero film, six use-case vignettes of 15 to 30 seconds, vertical, square and horizontal versions of every cut, edits for five platforms, and voiceover and on-screen text in three languages. Real product footage came from a two-day shoot, CGI carried the interface, and generated scenes carried the world around the device.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio's iFlytek AnyPin case study page, read 10 September 2026, figures as the case page publishes them, one engagement, no method statement.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/work/iflytek-anypin">iFlytek AnyPin case study</a> sets out the full build. Generated footage can still overstate a product, and the tool does not take the blame for it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A UK advertising regulator upheld 22 complaints against an ad for a robot toy, finding that footage which appeared to show real or AI-generated puppies gave the impression the product was highly realistic, and ruled that the advertiser must not misleadingly represent or exaggerate the functionality and appearance of its products.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: UK advertising regulator ruling, 25 March 2026, an upheld investigation under the code's misleading advertising, substantiation and exaggeration rules.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same regulator's guidance holds that the advertising code is media-neutral: the rules apply regardless of how the content was created, and the advertiser remains responsible for any AI output used in its ads.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: UK advertising regulator guidance, 11 June 2026, published guidance for advertisers on AI-generated content.</p>

  <p>At least one Chinese marketplace polices the same failure in product stills.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A Chinese marketplace became the first to publish a rule aimed specifically at AI fake product images, naming four failure categories: product material or style that does not match the real item, distorted or exaggerated product effects, an obvious cutout and paste look, and bodies or scenes that break physical laws.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Economic Information Daily (Jingji Cankao Bao), 27 March 2025, reporting the platform's own rule announcement and its detection-model enforcement.</p>

  <p>At volume, production practice moves the product accuracy into the model, trained on the product line itself.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A self-serve content platform with models trained per product line went live in eight weeks for a consumer electronics brand's North American teams and outside agencies, and produced thousands of brand assets a week after launch.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio's HiSense case study page, read 10 September 2026, figures as the case page publishes them, one engagement, no method statement.</p>

  <p>That eight weeks measures a platform going live, not a launch content build. The <a href="/work/hisense">HiSense case study</a> covers the deployment.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/solutions/manufacturers">manufacturers solutions page</a> describes launch imagery made before physical units exist. Pair any such image with two rules from this page. An image offered as device output has to be device output, and a radio device advertised in the US before FCC authorization carries the not-authorized statement. The <a href="/services/design/video-production">video production service</a> covers the capture side of the split.</p>

