  <h2>Questions about consumer electronics launch content</h2>

  <h3>What content does a consumer electronics launch need?</h3>

  <p>A hero film with use-case cut-downs in every ratio and language, a marketplace listing set with main images, a white-background image, detail pages, a specification chart, banners and short video, plus packaging and in-hand stills, interface renders, spec callouts, and certification mark and energy label images. Every asset shows a device making claims, so each has to match the real product.</p>

  <h3>Can product screens be simulated in ads?</h3>

  <p>No rule read for this page addresses the "screen images simulated" line itself. The rulings turn on presentation. A UK regulator upheld a complaint in 2024 over a night image offered as footage from a dash cam when it was a stock photo. In the US, an undisclosed mock-up presented as proof viewers can see is deceptive, and fine print may not fix a misleading visual.</p>

  <h3>How should certification marks appear in product images?</h3>

  <p>Only as the product holds them. In China, products on the compulsory certification catalog must be certified and marked before sale, and the mark must be used correctly in advertising and product introductions. In the US, the FCC logo is voluntary for devices authorized by supplier's declaration. The CE marking is a manufacturer's declaration, not an approval. This is production practice, not legal advice.</p>

  <h3>Which parts of a tech launch can be AI-generated?</h3>

  <p>Context, not proof. Lifestyle scenes, environments and backdrops can be generated. Footage offered as what the device does, performance demonstrations, and the marks and labels it holds come from the real product. A UK regulator upheld 22 complaints in 2026 over footage that appeared to show real or AI-generated puppies, and the advertiser stays responsible for AI output.</p>

  <h3>How long does a launch content build take?</h3>

  <p>No benchmark turned up in the sources checked for this page. The calendar depends on the gates in the build plan: test evidence, certification and authorization, and the energy class, then capture, generation and cut-downs. The iFlytek AnyPin case page reports "weeks, not months" for one hybrid build. That describes one engagement, not a standard.</p>
